hello and welcome to another week of  nothing new on breath of the wild too  wow even saying there's no new news on  the sequel feels old at this point  but while we've been wandering around  this no news desert like a bunch of  hype-starved  nomads there have been quite a few  videos made by quite a few different  creators across youtube space that have  served to voice the opinions of said  creators regarding what we would like to  see in the sequel to breath of the wild  you know in the case that it should ever  release i haven't really taken part in  these kinds of wishlist style videos  except for when i was  a part of this video from the legendary  hyrule gamer but that was quite a while  ago  so i figured what the heck it's been  long enough let's talk about my thoughts  regarding the sequel since i don't think  i've ever given you guys my complete  opinions on the matter  but we're gonna switch it up a bit we're  not necessarily gonna talk about what i  want to see happen but rather what i  don't want to see happen  so welcome to my list of the seven  possible ways that the sequel to breath  of the wild could be ruined  let's get right to it first up i would  be disappointed to see them use the  exact same map with no geographical  changes for breath of the wild 2.  even though it is pretty likely that  they will in fact be introducing new  areas and such  i mean just look at the caverns from the  trailer the reason why i mentioned this  is because mr aonuma did directly state  that they wanted to return to the same  hyrule from breath of the wild  meaning there are probably plans in  place to either use  all or large portions of the current map  of hyrule  if for some reason nintendo does not  include any new areas then we'd be stuck  exploring the same map we've already  been exploring for the past  four years now and unlikely as it is i  think everyone would be decently  disappointed in that  this does beg the question though where  would the new ares be located  my personal opinion on the matter is a  further expanded underground area  actually  and you can gather a bit more info on  that opinion of mine with a previous  theory video i did which i will link to  in the description if you're interested  i do hope though that no matter what new  areas are introduced they won't take  away from the free roam aspect that  breath of the wild is famous for  secondly i'd be disappointed if they  limited water movement  again like they did in breath of the  wild you see ever since the olden days  of a link to the past  underwater traversal has been a staple  of the legend of zelda series  and in majora's mask completely  three-dimensional underwater swimming  was introduced and has appeared in the  game's following twilight princess and  skyward sword  with the exception of the wind waker but  wind waker made up for it by including  you know an entire ocean so i ain't mad  in breath of the wild i legitimately did  not enjoy swimming and would actually  avoid getting into the water at all  costs if i didn't absolutely have to  and this was for some pretty good  reasons too not only can you not do  anything under the surface of the water  but you also move  cripplingly slowly and expend a hefty  amount of stamina whenever you try to  swim  not slowly ultimately the best thing you  can do in the game to enhance water  movement even a little bit is equip some  armor that makes you swim a bit faster  but also makes you spend stamina faster  as a result making it not really a buff  at all in the sequel if they keep water  travel the same way i'll be decently  disappointed  even if they still don't want to include  underwater traversal for whatever reason  at least allow the players some way to  enhance water movement like i don't know  water walking boots or the ability to  make ice platforms  i mean quicker forming flat ice  platforms or actual true water speed  buff that isn't also cancer to your  stamina wheel something to make  water great again thirdly and this is a  pretty common one but i figured i'd  mention it here with my own thoughts as  well  i'll be disappointed if they keep weapon  durability the same  not only do weapons break in like 20  hits in breath of the wild but once they  gone  they gone there's no saving or restoring  a weapon's life once it has  died in battle breath of the wild was  the first zelda game to format its  gameplay after open world adventure  games like skyrim so it does make sense  that changeable weapons and durabilities  and such would be copied from these  kinds of games but do you know what  those games also  include in addition to durability repair  what a concept i mean come on why does  the weapon have to shatter worse than my  heart on valentine's day 2015 whenever  its durability runs out  why not just sit unusable in your bags  or decrease its damage until you can get  it repaired at a blacksmith or something  this is one of the most common tropes in  open world video games and it's one that  i think  should 100 be implemented in breath of  the wild if the breakable weapons return  which by the way is up in the air  currently since link is shown to already  have the master sword in the trailer i  guess we'll just have to see what  happens  number four is about quest rewards i'm  assuming there will be quests and side  quests in the sequel as there have been  with  literally every other zelda game so far  and in so doing i'd be very disappointed  if there are still no  real unique quest rewards think about it  in all of breath of the wild  across all of its many side quests dlc  quests not included there's only one  quest that i can remember  that actually gives you a unique quest  reward and that is the quest to help out  with the citizens of gerudo town and  obtain the thunderhelm actually now that  i think about it there is one other  quest that lets you get the fish pants  from zora's domain but it's literally  just those two quests okay  every other quest is like oh man thanks  for helping here's  soup or here's an apple or here's  exactly three mushrooms  it's like yeah that's cool thanks for  the mushroom but i could have just  picked some of those up in the woods  over there but yeah cool thanks  there needs to be more actual meaningful  unique quest rewards in the sequel like  actual relics or gadgets that change or  augment the way you play the game  or unique weapons or armor pieces one of  the best examples of how this should  work would be like the masks in majora's  mask  just more in the fifth place again this  is a more common belief to share but  i'll be  extremely disappointed if dungeons do  not return  in the sequel and i don't mean more  little mini dungeons  i mean actual in-depth stylized ominous  beautiful  expansive innovative intricate  puzzle-filled classic zelda dungeons  sorry i get a little passionate about  this one because even though technically  speaking breath of the wild did have a  large portion of dungeon content more  than the previous games  it was all split up and scattered  throughout the map in forms of little  minis called shrines and a couple  slightly less minis called divine beasts  that were each pretty creative but still  way too short i'd rather see the return  of a few  large dungeons than a few hundred minis  even if it means doing away with the  mini dungeons entirely if that makes  sense  6. i will be disappointed if ganondorf  is once again  a mindless monster in the sequel one of  the biggest things i always felt was  missing from breath of the wild and  preventing it from feeling like a truly  true zelda game in its hearty heart was  its lack of a villainy villain  and no putting monster ganon doesn't  really qualify as a villain  he's more of a force of nature if you  will a mindless enemy that's just  bent on destruction destruction of what  you ask  oh you know just everything really in  general i think he's upset about life to  be honest which i mean ditto but you  don't see me blowing up castles  he doesn't speak and he doesn't think he  just grosses you out and screams when he  dies  we have a huge opportunity with  ganondorf that was presented to us in  the trailer  and that is that he could once again be  an actual intelligent villain and give  that spark of life to the sequel that i  and many others always felt was missing  from breath of the wild he could also  hydrate  point number six point five six and a  half if ganondorf doesn't hydrate we  will all be disappointed  finally in seventh place i will be  sorely disappointed  if link speaks now this is a more  divisive topic since there are more  people asking for link to audibly speak  these days than there ever have been  before but hear me out  it's not like link doesn't already speak  canonically in the games  he's never actually been mute as some  people would call him but rather is just  one of those slower to speaking quicker  to listen types of people i mean how  else do people think the dialogue  options have worked in the games clearly  link does actually talk we just simply  don't hear it  and listen guys we're about to go big  brain mode here this is the reason why  nintendo normally shies away from having  their protagonists speak  speaking and speech patterns are one of  the biggest ways to judge an  individual's character it's just true i  mean most of you listening to me right  now have an image of my character in  your mind that you've only been able to  develop by listening to me talk  you probably think i'm some devilishly  handsome buff guy when in reality  actually that's pretty accurate anyway  if you omit someone's speech from being  heard their character can still be  judged but based off of what they do  instead of what they say it's a genius  concept actually in link's case  his true character resounds with every  player individually based off of what  that player has link  do in that way it's virtually impossible  for anyone to dislike link since his  character will inevitably reflect  well you now that doesn't mean he's a  hollow character by any means i mean  this isn't disney  after all there are still canonical  things link does that make parts of his  character undeniable  for instance he's a hero which implies  several details regarding his character  like bravery and chivalry and nobility  that's not something that's debatable  unless you don't complete the game but  then it's not canon anyway but anyway i  said all of that to say  that if he does actually begin to  audibly talk in full sentences in the  sequel  everything that has been built up into  link's character over the past 35 years  that the franchise has been alive that's  unique to every single zelda fan will  immediately be put in jeopardy simply  because he would now have a voice  i mean imagine if the voice actor for  link ends up giving a poor performance  now all of a sudden we'd have his  canonical voice be annoying for the rest  of time  again this isn't disney and that's my  list of the seven ways to potentially  ruin breath of the wild too  what did you guys think was i just  incoherently rambling the whole time and  being too much of a brat or am i on to  something with these points  let me know what you think in the  comments below also huge thanks for  watching and still watching if you're  hearing me say this right now  please consider dropping a like below to  get the video spread around on youtube  space and subscribe if you haven't  already for much more nintendo content  just like this  also also humongous thanks to my  supporters please give a warm and hearty  welcome to  likkan confused hylian and clint 29 who  have all generously decided to support  the channel and join the ranks of my  elite bandit crew  if you watching are interested in doing  the same and helping the next video come  to life  feel free to check out that join button  located below or follow the links to my  patreon page in the 